# Test: the vrrp plugin auto-loads on a shared `interface` config that carries
# ZERO vrrp groups, and stays idle (spec-vrrp-5 AC-6, umbrella AC-6 / A-4).
#
# VALIDATES: AC-6's darwin-observable half -- with interfaces configured but no
# vrrp group, (1) `ze config validate` accepts the config, proving the vrrp
# plugin is wired to the shared `interface` root (ConfigRoots ["interface"],
# register.go) and its in-process verifier extracts ZERO GroupSpecs and returns
# nil on the idle path (verifyVRRPConfigSections -> extractGroupSpecs -> [] ->
# validateGroups([], "netlink") == nil); and (2) `ze doctor --json` emits NO
# vrrp diagnostic, proving the loaded-but-idle plugin's config-sanity check
# (checkVRRPConfigSanity -> diagnoseSections, doctor.go) returns early on
# len(specs)==0 and never cries wolf on the vast majority of deployments that
# share the `interface` root but never configure VRRP.
# PREVENTS: a regression where the zero-group boundary is mishandled -- the vrrp
# verifier or doctor check erroring / firing on an interface tree that has no
# vrrp block at all (which would break `ze config validate` and `ze doctor` for
# every ze deployment, since every interface config auto-loads this plugin).
#
# SCOPE: AC-6 also asserts the RUNNING daemon stays idle -- `show vrrp` reports
# zero instances through the real CLI->proxy->engine path, and no macvlan
# devices / raw sockets exist. Those clauses need a booted daemon, which needs
# the netlink interface backend (macvlan + AF_PACKET/raw sockets). On darwin the
# interface component that vrrp depends on cannot apply config at all ("interface
# management not supported on darwin"), so the daemon cannot boot here. The
# daemon-level idle clauses are therefore validated on Linux: the empty-list
# `show vrrp` path in test/vrrp/vrrp-show.ci (empty selector result through the
# real dispatch path) and the "zero groups -> no kernel macvlan" clause in
# test/vrrp/vrrp-instance-up.ci (post-teardown kernel readback). This file is the
# darwin-runnable, kernel-free portion, so `./le functional vrrp-test` covers AC-6 on the
# dev box while the QEMU needs-linux target covers the live behavior.

# --- Interfaces configured, ZERO vrrp groups: config validates. --------------
# An ipv4 address on a unit with NO vrrp augment. If the vrrp plugin were not
# wired to the `interface` root, a `vrrp { ... }` block would be an unknown field
# (proven by the group-present cases in vrrp-config.ci); here the block is absent
# and the plugin must accept the tree as a legitimate idle deployment.
tmpfs=vrrp-idle.conf:terminator=EOF_IDLE
interface {
    backend netlink;
    ethernet eth0 {
        unit 0 {
            ipv4 {
                address [ 192.0.2.251/24 ];
            }
        }
    }
    ethernet eth1 {
        unit 0 {
            ipv6 {
                address [ 2001:db8::2/64 ];
            }
        }
    }
}
EOF_IDLE

cmd=foreground:seq=1:exec=ze config validate vrrp-idle.conf:timeout=60s:exit=0
expect=stdout:contains=configuration valid

# --- Same idle config through `ze doctor`: the vrrp check stays quiet. --------
# A separate file-level command block would collide with the exit-code and
# accumulated-stdout model (see vrrp-doctor.ci header); this file keeps validate
# above and doctor here because both are exit-0 and neither prints a vrrp code on
# the healthy idle path, so the reject patterns below are meaningful.
# No exit-code assertion on doctor, and that is the point. `ze doctor` reports on
# the WHOLE machine, so its exit status carries every check's verdict:
# checkInterfaces (internal/component/doctor/checks_linux.go) emits
# doctor-iface-missing at ERROR severity for a configured ethernet device with no
# /sys/class/net entry, and eth0/eth1 above exist on no dev box, on no CI runner,
# and not in the QEMU VM (which has eth0 alone). Asserting exit 0 therefore
# asserted the host's NIC inventory, which is why this file was red everywhere.
# seq=1 keeps its own exit=0: `ze config validate` judges the config and nothing
# else. The property under test here is that the VRRP check stays quiet, so the
# payload assertion below proves doctor ran and produced its JSON and the rejects
# prove no vrrp code is in it.
cmd=foreground:seq=2:exec=ze doctor --json vrrp-idle.conf:timeout=60s
expect=stdout:contains=schema-version
reject=stdout:pattern=doctor-vrrp-config-invalid
reject=stdout:pattern=doctor-vrrp-backend-unusable
reject=stdout:pattern=doctor-vrrp-raw-socket
